window.DB_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tables, Rows & Keys","f":"p1-tables-and-keys.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which two properties does PostgreSQL enforce for every primary key?","o":["Stable and small","Unique and not null","Sequential and indexed","Numeric and positive"],"a":1,"w":"The database checks uniqueness (through the key's unique index) and rejects NULLs. Stability is a design choice nothing enforces."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tables, Rows & Keys","f":"p1-tables-and-keys.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"INSERT INTO orders (customer_id) VALUES (999) fails with \"violates foreign key constraint\". What does that mean?","o":["Order 999 already exists","customer_id must be unique in orders","The orders table is locked","No row in customers has customer_id 999"],"a":3,"w":"A foreign key requires every value in the child column to exist as a key in the parent table. The DETAIL line says exactly that: Key (customer_id)=(999) is not present in table \"customers\"."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tables, Rows & Keys","f":"p1-tables-and-keys.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is email a poor primary key for a users table, even if every user has a unique one?","o":["Users change their email, and every row, cache and URL that stores the key would have to change too","Text columns can't be primary keys","PostgreSQL can't index text","Emails can't be unique"],"a":0,"w":"Keys get copied everywhere. A key that changes turns one update into many. Keep email as a UNIQUE column and use a surrogate key."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tables, Rows & Keys","f":"p1-tables-and-keys.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the most natural primary key for order_items(order_id, product_id, quantity, unit_cents) , where a product appears at most once per order?","o":["product_id","order_id","(order_id, product_id)","quantity"],"a":2,"w":"Neither foreign key is unique on its own: an order has many lines, and a product is in many orders. The pair is unique, and as the key it also enforces \"one line per product per order\"."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Tables, Rows & Keys","f":"p1-tables-and-keys.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You run SELECT * FROM customers twice and get the rows in different orders. Why?","o":["The table is corrupted","A table is a set; without ORDER BY the database may return rows in any order","The primary key index is missing","Another session is sorting the table"],"a":1,"w":"Rows have no inherent order. Updates, VACUUM and parallel or synchronised scans can all change the order rows come back in. Only ORDER BY guarantees one."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Your First SELECT","f":"p2-first-select.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does SELECT name FROM products WHERE discount_pct = NULL return?","o":["The products with no discount","An error","No rows: the comparison is unknown for every row","Every row"],"a":2,"w":"Comparing anything with NULL using = yields unknown, and WHERE keeps only true rows. Use IS NULL."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Your First SELECT","f":"p2-first-select.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does SELECT price_cents / 100.0 AS price FROM products WHERE price > 10 fail?","o":["WHERE is evaluated before SELECT, so the alias price doesn't exist yet","You can't divide in a SELECT list","Aliases must be in double quotes","WHERE can't compare decimals"],"a":0,"w":"The logical order is FROM, WHERE, SELECT, ORDER BY, LIMIT. ORDER BY can use the alias; WHERE has to repeat the expression."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Your First SELECT","f":"p2-first-select.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In PostgreSQL, where do NULLs appear with ORDER BY discount_pct DESC ?","o":["Last","Randomly","They are excluded","First, because NULL sorts as larger than every value"],"a":3,"w":"PostgreSQL treats NULL as the largest value: last when ascending, first when descending. NULLS FIRST / NULLS LAST overrides it."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Your First SELECT","f":"p2-first-select.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"x NOT IN (1, 2, NULL) is true for which values of x?","o":["Every value except 1 and 2","None: the comparison with NULL makes the whole condition unknown or false","Only NULL","Only 3"],"a":1,"w":"It expands to x <> 1 AND x <> 2 AND x <> NULL. The last term is always unknown, so the AND can be false or unknown but never true."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Your First SELECT","f":"p2-first-select.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is false AND NULL ?","o":["NULL (unknown)","true","false","An error"],"a":2,"w":"An AND with a false side is false whatever the other side is. Only when the answer depends on the unknown side is it unknown."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Disks, Files & Pages","f":"p3-disks-and-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the size of a PostgreSQL page, the unit it reads and writes in?","o":["512 bytes","8 KB","16 KB","1 MB"],"a":1,"w":"PostgreSQL uses 8 KB blocks (SHOW block_size returns 8192). InnoDB's default is 16 KB."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Disks, Files & Pages","f":"p3-disks-and-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Roughly how long does one random 8 KB read take on a 7,200 rpm hard disk?","o":["8 nanoseconds","80 microseconds","1 second","About 8 milliseconds: a seek plus waiting for the platter to rotate"],"a":3,"w":"Average seek is around 4 ms and half a rotation at 7,200 rpm is about 4.2 ms, so roughly 120 random reads per second."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Disks, Files & Pages","f":"p3-disks-and-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why doesn't an NVMe drive's \"500,000 IOPS\" apply to a single query walking a B-tree index?","o":["Each read depends on the previous one, so they happen one at a time at full latency, not in parallel","B-trees are stored in RAM","NVMe drives can't do random reads","PostgreSQL disables NVMe queues"],"a":0,"w":"The headline IOPS figure needs many requests in flight. A tree walk can't ask for the child page until it has read the parent, so it sees the queue-depth-1 latency."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Disks, Files & Pages","f":"p3-disks-and-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A query's second run is 50× faster than its first. What is the most likely reason?","o":["PostgreSQL compiled the query","The index was rebuilt","The pages it needs are now cached in shared_buffers and the OS page cache","The disk spun up"],"a":2,"w":"The first run paid for reads from storage; the second found the pages in memory. EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) shows the shift from \"read\" to \"hit\"."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Disks, Files & Pages","f":"p3-disks-and-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On disk, what is a PostgreSQL table?","o":["One row per file","A file (split into 1 GB segments) under the data directory, made of 8 KB pages","A single file holding every table in the database","A directory of CSV files"],"a":1,"w":"Each table and index has its own relation file; pg_relation_filepath() tells you where. Files over 1 GB continue in .1, .2 … segments."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Data Types","f":"p4-data-types.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does SELECT 2147483647::integer + 1 do in PostgreSQL?","o":["Returns 2147483648","Wraps around to -2147483648","Raises \"integer out of range\"","Returns NULL"],"a":2,"w":"PostgreSQL checks integer overflow and raises an error rather than wrapping. 2,147,483,647 is the largest 32-bit signed integer."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Data Types","f":"p4-data-types.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which type is best for storing order totals?","o":["numeric(12,2), or bigint holding cents","double precision","real","text"],"a":0,"w":"Both are exact. Floating-point types store binary fractions and accumulate rounding errors in sums."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Data Types","f":"p4-data-types.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does a timestamptz value store?","o":["The local time and the zone name","A string with an offset","The local time only","An absolute instant (in UTC), shown in each session's time zone"],"a":3,"w":"The name is misleading: no zone is stored. Input is converted to UTC, and output is converted to the session's TimeZone setting."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Data Types","f":"p4-data-types.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In PostgreSQL, how does varchar(255) compare with text for performance?","o":["varchar(255) is faster because it is bounded","They're the same; varchar(n) only adds a length check","text is stored outside the table and is slower","text can't be indexed"],"a":1,"w":"The documentation states there's no performance difference between text, varchar and varchar(n), apart from char(n)'s padding."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Data Types","f":"p4-data-types.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does '{\"id\": 7, \"id\": 8}'::jsonb return?","o":["An error: duplicate key","{\"id\": 7, \"id\": 8}","{\"id\": 8}","{\"id\": 7}"],"a":2,"w":"jsonb keeps only the last value for a duplicated key. The json type would have kept the input text exactly, duplicates included."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"JSON, CSV & Loading Data","f":"p5-json-csv.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In psql, what's the difference between COPY t FROM '/tmp/x.csv' and \\copy t FROM '/tmp/x.csv' ?","o":["None; \\copy is an alias","COPY reads the file on the database server; \\copy reads it on the client and streams it","\\copy is slower because it uses INSERTs","COPY only works for CSV"],"a":1,"w":"COPY with a file path is executed by the server process and needs server file access. \\copy is a psql command that sends the local file as COPY FROM STDIN."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"JSON, CSV & Loading Data","f":"p5-json-csv.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In PostgreSQL's CSV format, how is the line 7,,\"\" loaded into three text columns?","o":["'7', '', ''","'7', NULL, NULL","It's rejected as malformed","'7', NULL, '' (unquoted empty is NULL, quoted empty is an empty string)"],"a":3,"w":"By default CSV format treats an unquoted empty field as NULL and a quoted empty field \"\" as an empty string."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"JSON, CSV & Loading Data","f":"p5-json-csv.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the main reason one INSERT per row in autocommit mode is so slow?","o":["Every row pays a network round trip, statement parsing, and a commit that waits for a WAL flush","PostgreSQL locks the whole table for each insert","INSERT rebuilds the indexes each time","Autocommit disables the buffer pool"],"a":0,"w":"The actual insert takes microseconds. The per-statement overheads, especially the round trip and the durable commit, dominate."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"JSON, CSV & Loading Data","f":"p5-json-csv.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A field in a CSV file contains a line break inside double quotes. What follows?","o":["The file is invalid CSV","COPY splits it into two rows","It's valid: one record spans two physical lines, so line-based tools can break it","The line break is converted to a space"],"a":2,"w":"Quoted fields may contain newlines. A proper CSV parser keeps the record whole; wc -l and split don't know about quoting."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"JSON, CSV & Loading Data","f":"p5-json-csv.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why load an untrusted CSV into an all- text staging table first?","o":["Text columns load faster than any other type","The load can't fail on types, and you can validate, log rejects and transform with SQL before touching real tables","COPY only supports text columns","Staging tables are automatically indexed"],"a":1,"w":"One bad value makes a direct COPY fail entirely. Staging separates \"get the bytes in\" from \"decide what's valid\", and the final upsert is one atomic step."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Python & psycopg","f":"p6-python-psycopg.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which line is safe from SQL injection?","o":["cur.execute(f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '{email}'\")","cur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '%s'\" % email)","cur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = %s\", (email,))","cur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = \" + email)"],"a":2,"w":"Only the third passes the value as a parameter. The second uses Python's % operator, which formats the string before psycopg sees it."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Python & psycopg","f":"p6-python-psycopg.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why can't you write ORDER BY %s and pass a column name as a parameter?","o":["Parameters are values; the server must know identifiers to parse the statement, so the column name would be treated as a constant string","psycopg doesn't support ORDER BY","Column names must be integers","It works, but slowly"],"a":0,"w":"ORDER BY 'price_cents' sorts by a constant, which does nothing useful. Use sql.Identifier with an allow-list for dynamic identifiers."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Python & psycopg","f":"p6-python-psycopg.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A script runs cur.execute(\"UPDATE ...\") on a plain psycopg.connect() connection and then exits without committing. What happens to the update?","o":["It is committed automatically","It is committed when the server restarts","It is half-applied","It is rolled back: psycopg opened a transaction implicitly and it was never committed"],"a":3,"w":"psycopg 3 isn't in autocommit mode by default. Without commit() (or leaving a with-connect block normally), the transaction is discarded."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Python & psycopg","f":"p6-python-psycopg.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You need to export 50 million rows from Python without running out of memory. What do you use?","o":["cur.fetchall() on an ordinary cursor","A named (server-side) cursor, which fetches rows in batches","cur.fetchone() in a loop on an ordinary cursor","A larger LIMIT"],"a":1,"w":"An ordinary cursor receives the whole result when execute() returns, whatever fetch method you call afterwards. A named cursor keeps it on the server. COPY TO STDOUT is the other good option."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Python & psycopg","f":"p6-python-psycopg.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does the input x' OR '1'='1 do when formatted into WHERE email = '{input}' ?","o":["Matches nothing","Raises a syntax error","Makes the condition true for every row, so every customer is returned","Deletes the table"],"a":2,"w":"The query becomes WHERE email = 'x' OR '1'='1', and '1'='1' is always true. With a parameter, the whole input is one string compared with email and matches nothing."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Sets & Relational Algebra","f":"p7-sets-relational-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which SQL corresponds to the projection π dept_id (employees) in relational algebra?","o":["SELECT dept_id FROM employees","SELECT DISTINCT dept_id FROM employees","SELECT * FROM employees WHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL","SELECT count(dept_id) FROM employees"],"a":1,"w":"Algebraic projection returns a set, so duplicates are removed. Plain SELECT keeps duplicates (bag semantics)."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Sets & Relational Algebra","f":"p7-sets-relational-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"employees has 1,000 rows and departments has 20. How many rows does SELECT * FROM employees, departments return with no WHERE clause?","o":["1,000","1,020","20","20,000: a Cartesian product"],"a":3,"w":"Listing tables with commas and no join condition is R × S: every employee paired with every department."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Sets & Relational Algebra","f":"p7-sets-relational-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which query lists customers who ordered in 2025 but not in 2026?","o":["The 2025 customer IDs EXCEPT the 2026 customer IDs","The 2025 IDs INTERSECT the 2026 IDs","The 2025 IDs UNION the 2026 IDs","The 2025 IDs UNION ALL the 2026 IDs"],"a":0,"w":"Difference (R − S, EXCEPT in SQL) keeps rows of the first input that don't appear in the second."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Sets & Relational Algebra","f":"p7-sets-relational-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is UNION ALL usually faster than UNION ?","o":["It uses an index","It runs the two queries in parallel","It doesn't have to sort or hash all rows to remove duplicates","It returns fewer rows"],"a":2,"w":"UNION must find and remove duplicates across the combined result. UNION ALL just concatenates."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Sets & Relational Algebra","f":"p7-sets-relational-algebra.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does \"pushing a selection below a join\" mean?","o":["Running the WHERE clause after the SELECT list","Applying a filter that uses only one input's columns to that input before joining, so the join processes fewer rows","Replacing the join with a subquery","Adding an index on the join column"],"a":1,"w":"σ p (R ⋈ S) = R ⋈ σ p (S) when p mentions only S (for inner joins). Same result, smaller intermediate data."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Data-Size Arithmetic","f":"p8-data-size-math.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What fixed overhead does every PostgreSQL row carry before any column data?","o":["None","8 bytes","A 23-byte tuple header (padded to 24) plus a 4-byte line pointer","One full 8 KB page"],"a":2,"w":"The header holds MVCC information; the line pointer lives in the page's slot array. Together about 28 bytes per row."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Data-Size Arithmetic","f":"p8-data-size-math.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Columns declared as (boolean, bigint, boolean, bigint) waste space because…","o":["each bigint must start at a multiple of 8, so 7 padding bytes follow each boolean","booleans are stored as 8 bytes","PostgreSQL stores booleans in a separate file","bigints are compressed"],"a":0,"w":"Alignment padding. Declaring the bigints first and the booleans after removes it."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Data-Size Arithmetic","f":"p8-data-size-math.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Rows are 76 bytes including the line pointer. Roughly how many fit on an 8 KB PostgreSQL page?","o":["8","54","1,000","About 107"],"a":3,"w":"8,168 usable bytes ÷ 76 ≈ 107."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Data-Size Arithmetic","f":"p8-data-size-math.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Roughly how big is a B-tree index on a bigint column for 100 million rows?","o":["About 22 MB","About 2.2 GB","About 800 MB, exactly 8 bytes per row","About 22 GB"],"a":1,"w":"Each entry is about 20 bytes (8-byte index tuple header, 8-byte key, 4-byte line pointer), pages are 90% full: around 22 MB per million rows."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Data-Size Arithmetic","f":"p8-data-size-math.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A table is 300 GB and the server has 128 GB of RAM. Does it matter?","o":["Yes: the whole table must fit in RAM to be queried","No: RAM size never matters for databases","It depends on the working set: if queries mostly touch recent rows and index upper levels that fit in RAM, it can perform well","Only if it has no indexes"],"a":2,"w":"Databases cache pages, not tables. What matters is whether the pages your queries actually touch stay cached."}];
